00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.960
On water, the case on both sides is whether the next B.C. government

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.580
should tighten conservation before a strong El Niño.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.820
Advocates want that government to act

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.940
because a super El Niño could deepen drought.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.840
The World Meteorological Organization said Thursday

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.720
that central and eastern tropical Pacific surface water

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.780
is forecast to hit record warmth, topping out in December.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.340
Secretary General Celeste Salo said the pattern is locked in and building fast.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.220
With a near 100 percent chance it holds through February 2027,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.600
maybe the strongest since at least 1950.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.000
Corey Tull, chair of the BC Watershed Coalition, says winter should run milder,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.660
with more rain, a thinner mountain snowpack, and a weaker spring melt.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.760
She says a lot of watersheds already carry a deficit into 2027,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.340
so another poor snow year could stack on the drought.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:11.520
She lists tighter restrictions, higher bills for towns and farmers, streams too low for

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.120
fish and wildlife, and more fuel for wildfire.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.600
River Forecast Center numbers alongside her comments show high-level drought in BC

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.860
every year since 2015, and wider severe drought from 2022 through 2024.

